$DIR/main.p:28:2: cannot store &*c in package variable last, which outlives c
    &*c points into the object c refers to, which c keeps alive in Put. c
    (declared at main.p:26) is an owned[Conn]: the object it refers to lives
    only while c holds it, and is dropped when c is dropped at the end of its
    scope in Put, or released when it is the last reference. A pointer into the
    object, or an interface that lends it, is a view that may not outlive c.
    When Put returns, c is dropped and the object freed, but package variables
    live for the whole program, so package variable last would be left pointing
    at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store c itself, changing the type of last to match: an owned box moves
    there, and a shared or synced one is counted:
        last = c
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
